## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical concurrency control skills involving multiple producers, bounded buffers, and latency guarantees with a simple priority policy. The solution tests real-world challenges like starvation prevention and graceful shutdown coordination.

## Key Concepts

- Dual-queue architecture for priority separation
- Mutual exclusion and condition synchronization
- Backpressure mechanisms for flow control
- Aging algorithms to prevent starvation
- Graceful shutdown patterns with resource cleanup

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo sketch of data structures and worker loop
class Task { 
  constructor(priority) { 
    this.priority = priority; 
    this.timestamp = Date.now();
  } 
}

class PriorityQueue {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.highQueue = [];
    this.normalQueue = [];
    this.mutex = new Mutex();
    this.notEmpty = new ConditionVariable();
    this.notFull = new ConditionVariable();
    this.shutdown = false;
    this.agingThreshold = 5000; // 5 seconds
  }

  async enqueue(task) {
    await this.mutex.lock();
    while (this.size() >= this.capacity && !this.shutdown) {
      await this.notFull.wait();
    }

    if (task.priority === 'high') {
      this.highQueue.push(task);
    } else {
      this.normalQueue.push(task);
    }

    this.notEmpty.signal();
    this.mutex.unlock();
  }

  async dequeue() {
    await this.mutex.lock();
    while (this.size() === 0 && !this.shutdown) {
      await this.notEmpty.wait();
    }

    if (this.shutdown && this.size() === 0) {
      this.mutex.unlock();
      return null;
    }

    this.checkAging();

    let task;
    if (this.highQueue.length > 0) {
      task = this.highQueue.shift();
    } else if (this.normalQueue.length > 0) {
      task = this.normalQueue.shift();
    }

    this.notFull.signal();
    this.mutex.unlock();
    return task;
  }

  checkAging() {
    const now = Date.now();
    this.normalQueue = this.normalQueue.filter(task => {
      if (now - task.timestamp > this.agingThreshold) {
        this.highQueue.push(task);
        return false;
      }
      return true;
    });
  }

  size() {
    return this.highQueue.length + this.normalQueue.length;
  }

  async shutdown() {
    await this.mutex.lock();
    this.shutdown = true;
    this.notEmpty.broadcast();
    this.notFull.broadcast();
    this.mutex.unlock();
  }
}
```

## Test Plan

### Latency Testing
- Measure end-to-end processing time for high vs. normal priority tasks
- Verify high-priority tasks consistently achieve lower latency
- Test with varying load levels to validate priority guarantees

### Starvation Testing
- Generate continuous high-priority load and monitor normal task processing
- Verify aging mechanism promotes aged normal tasks
- Measure maximum wait time for normal tasks under sustained high load

### Graceful Shutdown Testing
- Initiate shutdown while tasks are processing
- Verify all in-flight tasks complete before worker termination
- Test with producers blocked on backpressure
- Confirm no resource leaks or deadlocks

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic topology changes without downtime, while guaranteeing per-key in-order delivery and exactly-once semantics under backpressure. It also probes practical test planning for migration safety and performance.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime topology changes with zero-downtime
- Per-key state migration and sequence tracking
- Bounded buffers and backpressure signaling
- Exactly-once semantics across reconfiguration
- Versioned topology and safe handoff

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch for key handoff
function migrateKey(key, oldWorker, newWorker) {
  // Phase 1: Drain in-flight items
  oldWorker.pauseKey(key);
  await oldWorker.flushKey(key);

  // Phase 2: Transfer state atomically
  const state = oldWorker.getState(key);
  const sequence = oldWorker.getSequence(key);
  newWorker.restoreState(key, state, sequence);

  // Phase 3: Resume processing
  newWorker.resumeKey(key);
}
```

## Test Plan
1. **Unit Tests**: Verify consistent hashing assignment and state transfer accuracy
2. **Integration Tests**: Test end-to-end reconfiguration with various traffic patterns
3. **Load Tests**: Validate backpressure handling under high throughput with bounded buffers
4. **Chaos Tests**: Random failures during migration to ensure data consistency
5. **Performance Benchmarks**: Measure reconfiguration latency and throughput impact

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of ordering guarantees across a dynamically resizing worker pool, per-entity isolation, and practical backpressure in high-throughput streaming systems.

## Key Concepts
- Per-entity FIFO queues with monotonic sequence numbers for strict ordering
- Global bounded buffer with admission control
- Backpressure discipline that defers or drops lower-priority event kinds when the buffer is saturated
- Dynamic worker pool with consistent entity-to-worker mapping for stability
- Late-event handling policy to maintain liveness without breaking per-entity order

## Code Example
```javascript
class Router {
  constructor(bound, workerPool) {
    this.bound = bound;
    this.workers = workerPool;
    this.globalBuffer = [];
    this.entityQueues = new Map();
    this.entitySeq = new Map();
  }

  route(event) {
    if (this.globalBuffer.length >= this.bound) {
      if (event.priority < HIGH) return this.defer(event);
      this.drop(event);
    }

    const seq = this.entitySeq.get(event.entity_id) ?? 0;
    this.entitySeq.set(event.entity_id, seq + 1);

    if (!this.entityQueues.has(event.entity_id)) {
      this.entityQueues.set(event.entity_id, []);
    }

    this.entityQueues.get(event.entity_id).push({ ...event, seq });
    this.assignWorker(event.entity_id);
  }

  isLate(event) {
    return Date.now() - event.ts > LATENESS_THRESHOLD;
  }

  drop(event) { /* log and discard */ }
  defer(event) { /* requeue with backoff */ }
}
```

## Test Plan
1. **Ordering Guarantee**: Spawn N workers, emit out-of-order events for the same entity, verify output maintains sequence order.
2. **Backpressure**: Fill the buffer and confirm lower-priority events are deferred or dropped while higher-priority events are admitted.
3. **Lateness Drop**: Send events with timestamps older than L; verify they are discarded.
4. **Worker Resize**: Add/remove workers mid-stream and confirm no ordering violations for any entity.
5. **Throughput**: Measure events/sec processed under steady-state load.
6. **Recovery**: Simulate worker failure and verify no events are lost or duplicated.

